WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.599 --> 00:00:10.719
In a Chicago school, one kindergartner fretted
to a friend that he wouldn't be able to get

00:00:10.719 --> 00:00:14.579
his spare underpants from his cubby in case
he had an accident.

00:00:14.579 --> 00:00:15.579
Why?

00:00:15.579 --> 00:00:20.730
Staff at the prestigious, private Catherine
Cook School had opted to deny five and six-year-olds

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:24.439
access to their cubbies in order to school
them on privilege.

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:29.079
Later, kindergartners were selectively denied
access to the playground equipment so they

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:31.379
might experience discrimination.

00:00:31.379 --> 00:00:35.440
If one student did this to another, we would
rightly call it bullying.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:41.030
But when parents raised concerns, the principle
told them, "Catherine Cook is a current century

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:42.260
progressive school.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:47.100
Educators who regard themselves as socially
conscious can get so invested in agenda, that

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.539
they lose sight of the real kids in front
of them."

00:00:49.539 --> 00:00:54.440
Look, the debate is not over whether students
should be taught about discrimination and

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:58.289
privilege, they should, but good teaching
requires judgment.

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:03.299
Kindergartners don't benefit from experiencing
violation to principles they don't yet understand.

00:01:03.299 --> 00:01:08.799
Educators need to find responsible ways to
introduce students to our shared values.

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:14.000
How do you think we should teach students
about charged social issues like inequality?

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.640
Let us know in your comments.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.859
Also, let us know what other topics you'd
like our scholars to cover in 60 seconds.

00:01:20.859 --> 00:01:25.270
And be sure to "Like" and subscribe for more
research and videos from AEI.

